# Revenue Engine Conventions

## Naming and style

- Use full, readable names for fields and dependencies (`revenueJournalEntriesService`, not `service`).
- Follow existing suffixes: `*Record`, `*Service`, `*Strategy`, `*Context`, `*Constants`.
- Prefer explicit names over abbreviations in methods and variables.

## Domain models

- Use **mutable classes with getters/setters** for workflow, batch, or line context objects built during processing.
- Use **records** only for small immutable DTOs or value objects.
- Build context objects in one place (typically a utility/builder method); populate all fields there, including derived values (currency, rounding precision, segments).
- Do not unpack context fields into method parameters when they already live on the context. Pass the context and read fields inside the method.

```java
// ❌ BAD — redundant parameters already on context
calculateAmount(lineContext, perPeriodAmount, totalAmount, currencyPrecision);

// ✅ GOOD — only pass what varies per call
calculateAmount(lineContext, perPeriodAmount);
```

## Package and class structure

```
{Feature}/
├── {Feature}Service.java           # orchestrates the workflow
├── {Feature}UtilityService.java    # shared helpers only
├── {Feature}Constants.java
├── model/                          # context and local DTOs
└── strategy/                       # variant-specific logic (when applicable)
    ├── {Feature}Strategy.java
    └── *Strategy.java
```

- **Orchestrator** (`*Service`): loads data, loops, delegates, persists.
- **Utility** (`*UtilityService`): shared setup, indexing, resolution, and context building — not variant-specific business rules.
- **Strategy**: variant-specific formulas and record building; selected via `supports(...)` + `process(...)`.

## Abstractions

- Do **not** add thin wrapper methods around services the caller already has.
- Do **not** extract one- or two-line helpers that should stay inline unless reused in multiple places.
- Do **not** replace maintainable SQL aggregation with in-memory aggregation when SQL already exists in the service layer.
- Minimize scope — only change what the task requires.

## Workflow and batch context

- Read cross-cutting workflow state (open period, tenant, batch id) from a dedicated context service or holder.
- Initialize and publish context once at workflow start; avoid threading the same value through every layer as a duplicate source of truth.

## Data access

- Prefer composite accessors that return related records in one call over multiple separate lookups in a loop.
- Avoid N+1: use batch queries + in-memory grouping (`findByIds`, `groupBy*`) instead of per-item fetches.
- Keep aggregation SQL in the persistence/service layer; upper layers consume the results.

## Financial amounts

- Use `BigDecimal` with `RoundingMode.HALF_UP` for monetary calculations.
- Resolve decimal precision from context (populated at build time from currency). Use `// TODO:` + a sensible default until currency setup is implemented.
- In multi-period allocation loops, let the **last period absorb rounding remainder** rather than rounding it independently.

## Comments

- Document non-obvious business formulas in method Javadoc.
- Use `// TODO:` for deferred integration points. Do not comment obvious code.
